      <p class="bd-casestudy__lead">A University City research facility had documented dozens of bird strikes per migration season on its south-facing curtain wall — a three-story stretch of floor-to-ceiling glass that reflected the tree canopy across the street.</p>
      <p class="bd-casestudy__body">The building's facilities team had been aware of the problem for years but was constrained by two competing requirements: the architectural specification required the glass to read as clear from the exterior, and the sustainability committee required a certified bird-safe solution. Bird Divert UV Dot Markers satisfied both. The near-invisible UV film maintained the appearance of the curtain wall from the exterior while delivering ABC-certified collision prevention across all three floors of at-risk glass.</p>
      <p class="bd-casestudy__body">Window Film Philadelphia completed the installation across three floors during a single two-day window using lift equipment coordinated with building facilities. Post-installation monitoring during the following fall migration season recorded a dramatic reduction in collisions on the treated glass compared with documented prior-season frequency. The installation documentation was incorporated into the building's LEED recertification submission.</p>
